{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## IMPLEMENTATION BLOCKED — resolved brief conflicts with LANDED epic dbca-97ac-ad96-4d6d (reporting up, NOT forcing past)\n\nExecuting the held-out-TDD Gerrit stack. Setup done (worktree `errorcode-taxonomy` off origin/main @ 5ae9eb72, session-log 59a7-cb88-f2f9-4148, epic description remediated with an `## Acceptance Criteria` checklist to clear the P1 readiness BLOCK). Before claiming/writing any code I re-based every fix against the ACTUAL current tree and found the brief was authored against a **pre-dbca** state. **dbca-97ac-ad96-4d6d is now CLOSED/landed** (commits `ae4f6c1a` \"Classify workflow read caller errors precisely\", `f854924b` \"Classify unknown scripted step as invalid_input\") and already resolves part of THIS epic with a DIFFERENT, documented answer. No `--force` used; nothing claimed; nothing pushed.\n\n### What dbca already landed (origin/main today)\n`error_code_for` branch 6 is now `_workflow_caller_code(exc)`:\n- `WorkflowNotFoundError` → **`not_found`**\n- `WorkflowParseError` / `WorkflowValidationError` / `WorkflowVersionError` / `WorkflowUnknownStepError` → **`invalid_input`**\n- bare `WorkflowError` base and bare `LLMError` → **`llm_unavailable`** (deliberately pinned)\n\n`KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` already gained `not_found`, `invalid_input`, `response_too_large`. This is enforced by `tests/interfaces/contracts/test_workflow_error_codes_dbca.py` (**9 tests, all passing**), including `test_execute_base_and_outage_still_llm_unavailable` which asserts `WorkflowError(\"…\")` and bare `LLMError(…)` → `llm_unavailable`, and is documented as a committed contract in `docs/mcp-reference.md` (\"a workflow that was found but will not parse/lint/migrate is delivered as `invalid_input` … a genuine LLM-backend unavailability — including one raised while a workflow EXECUTE step runs — still resolves to `llm_unavailable`\").\n\n### The three concrete conflicts with the resolved brief\n1. **7d47 is unsatisfiable/redundant as specified.** The brief says: add a test proving `render_workflow` surfaces the WorkflowError family as **`command_failed`**. But dbca already makes `render_workflow` surface that family as **`not_found`/`invalid_input`** (load-time faults), with landed passing tests. 7d47's INTENT (stop mislabeling as `llm_unavailable`) is already met by dbca — via a MORE precise code than `command_failed`. dbca's title is literally \"MCP LLM-tier tools report caller-input and not-found failures as llm_unavailable\" — it IS 7d47's surface.\n2. **The epic's own prevention guardrail contradicts landed dbca.** The AC asks for an exhaustive `LLMError.__subclasses__()` → code test asserting \"every non-`LLMUnavailableError` → `command_failed`\". Applied literally that asserts `WorkflowNotFoundError → command_failed`, contradicting dbca's tested/`not_found`. The guardrail can only be green if it EXEMPTS the dbca-classified workflow caller-input subtree (asserts their `not_found`/`invalid_input` instead).\n3. **f75f would pre-decide the still-OPEN escalated ticket 551b.** The brief says f75f \"resolves the 73d8/551b tension\", but **551b is still `open`** (\"Editor missing-bundle WorkflowError: decide error code (escalated design)\"). The contested item is exactly the bare-`WorkflowError`-base classification: brief-literal wants `command_failed`; dbca's landed test + docs pin `llm_unavailable`. Choosing either unilaterally either reverts dbca's landed/documented contract (destructive, breaks 9 tests + docs) or pre-empts an open escalated design ticket.\n\n### What is CLEAN and unaffected (landable exactly as briefed once the bottom is resolved)\n- **0aee** — register `pinned_ticket_read_failed` in `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`. Zero dbca interaction.\n- **5f96** (Option B) — split the `coverage.llm_unavailable` boolean in `gate_dispatch.py` + `input_rejected` + 3 readers. Different subsystem (gate coverage), zero dbca interaction.\n- **fbc0** — `_structured_llm_failure` → `error_code_for(exc)`. Safe delegation; its non-outage taxonomy improvement rides f75f.\n- **f75f's uncontested core** — routing the `LLMRunnerError` subtree (`LLMInputRejectedError`, `LLMBudgetExhaustedError`, `ContextWindowExceededError`, `RunawayToolLoopError`, `StructuredOutputError`/`UnretryableOutputError`/`CompletionRecoveryError`) OFF `llm_unavailable` → `command_failed`. This is f75f's enumerated target and does NOT touch dbca's workflow taxonomy.\n\nThe conflict is ISOLATED to: the WorkflowError subtree + bare `LLMError`/`WorkflowError` bases (f75f), 7d47 in whole, and the exception→code half of the guardrail. Because f75f is the BOTTOM of the relation-chain stack and everything rides it, I cannot land the clean upper pieces without first resolving f75f's contested portion — so I have landed nothing.\n\n### Recommended reconciliation (non-destructive; consistent with the brief's PHILOSOPHY, needs your OK on scope)\n- **f75f**: narrow branch 7 to route ONLY the `LLMRunnerError` subtree → `command_failed` (+ `LLMUnavailableError`/`LLMConfigError` stay `llm_unavailable`). **Preserve dbca's workflow taxonomy untouched** (`not_found`/`invalid_input`; bare bases stay `llm_unavailable`). This fixes 100% of the real, observable mislabelings without reverting a landed/documented contract or pre-deciding 551b. (The brief's own rule — \"grow vocabulary only on observable need\" — already endorses dbca's finer codes over `command_failed`.)\n- **7d47**: close as **resolved-by-dbca** (superseded/duplicate) with a confirmatory read that `render_workflow` surfaces the family as NOT `llm_unavailable` — which the landed dbca tests already assert.\n- **Guardrail**: exhaustive `LLMError` subclass → code test that ENCODES the reconciled taxonomy (availability types → `llm_unavailable`; `LLMRunnerError` subtree → `command_failed`; dbca workflow caller-input subtree → `not_found`/`invalid_input`; bare bases → `llm_unavailable`) + the vocabulary-closure test (pins 0aee). This makes the class un-regressable AND stays green against dbca.\n- **551b**: leave to its own escalated-design resolution; f75f explicitly does not touch the bare-base question.\n\n**Decision requested:** approve this reconciliation (recommended), OR direct me to implement the brief literally and REVERT dbca's `not_found`/`invalid_input` taxonomy + its 9 landed tests + the `docs/mcp-reference.md` contract (a cross-epic revert I will not do without explicit instruction). I will hold here — no claim, no push — pending your call."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788061396204313236,"uuid":"58c4cc36-e2f9-4cce-9c6c-c829f9c10eab"}